  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "A tax credit is a dollar-for-dollar reduction of the income tax a taxpayer owes, offering a direct decrease in the final tax bill rather than just reducing taxable income. These credits can be refundable (resulting in a refund if the credit exceeds the tax owed) or nonrefundable (reducing liability only to zero). Common Usage Examples of Tax CreditsTax credits are designed to provide financial incentives or relief for specific activities: Child Tax Credit: Provides up to $2,000 per qualifying child to reduce tax liability. Earned Income Tax Credit (EITC): A refundable credit designed to aid low-to-moderate-income working individuals and families. American Opportunity Tax Credit (AOTC): Helps with education costs, offering up to $2,500 per eligible student. Child and Dependent Care Credit: Assists with the costs of caring for dependents so the taxpayer can work. Retirement Savings Contributions Credit: A \"saver's credit\" for low-to-moderate-income individuals contributing to retirement plans. Premium Tax Credit (PTC): Helps eligible individuals pay for health insurance through the marketplace. Electric Vehicle (EV) Tax Credit: Incentivizes the purchase of qualifying electric vehicles. Synonyms and Related Terms Tax Incentive Tax Reduction Tax Rebate (when refundable) Tax Relief Key DistinctionsUnlike tax deductions, which only reduce the amount of income subject to taxation, tax credits directly lower the amount of tax you owe. Types of Tax Credits Refundable: If the credit amount is greater than the tax owed, the excess is refunded to the taxpayer. Nonrefundable: The credit can only reduce tax liability to zero. Partially Refundable: A mix, where only a portion of the credit is refundable.",
